### Calendar API Tests

Exercises `/api/calendar` (events and breaks) with the VS Code REST Client.
Select the `local` or `deployed` environment; `baseUrl`, `adminUid` and
`adminPassword` are the same variables `toolchain.http` uses, set in the
Git-ignored `.vscode/settings.json`.

Run the requests in order. They write to that environment's real calendar, so
they use 2099 dates and "HTTP test" titles, and the last ones delete them.

### 1. Sign in (the JWT cookie is kept for the requests below)

# @name loginCalendar
POST {{baseUrl}}/authenticate
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json

{
  "uid": "{{adminUid}}",
  "password": "{{adminPassword}}"
}

### 2. Add an event: the body becomes an EventRequest, the answer is an EventView

# @name addCalendarEvent
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/calendar/add_event
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "HTTP test: sprint review",
  "date": "2099-01-05",
  "description": "Created by api/calendar.http",
  "type": "event",
  "period": "CSA"
}

### 3. Missing title: expect 400 {"message": "Invalid input: 'title' cannot be null or empty."}

POST {{baseUrl}}/api/calendar/add_event
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "date": "2099-01-05"
}

### 4. Read a date range

GET {{baseUrl}}/api/calendar/events/range?start=2099-01-01&end=2099-01-31

### 5. Filter (every parameter is optional)

GET {{baseUrl}}/api/calendar/events/filter?type=event&start=2099-01-01&end=2099-01-31

### 6. Edit the event from step 2 (the edit body names the title newTitle; fields left out keep their values)

PUT {{baseUrl}}/api/calendar/edit/{{addCalendarEvent.response.body.$.id}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "newTitle": "HTTP test: sprint review (moved)",
  "description": "Edited by api/calendar.http",
  "date": "2099-01-06",
  "period": "CSA"
}

### 7. Add events in bulk: expect created 2, failed 1 (the last one has no date)

POST {{baseUrl}}/api/calendar/add_events
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "events": [
    { "title": "HTTP test: unit 3 quiz", "date": "2099-01-07", "period": "CSA" },
    { "title": "HTTP test: retrospective", "date": "2099-01-08", "period": "CSA" },
    { "title": "HTTP test: no date" }
  ]
}

### 8. Import a Slack weekly plan: CalendarImportMsg makes 3 events (Mon, and a Tue - Wed check-in)

POST {{baseUrl}}/api/calendar/add
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "channel": "CRRJL1F1D",
  "text": "Week of 1/12/2099\n[Mon]: HTTP test: plan review\n[Tue - Wed]: *HTTP test: check-ins\n• bring your kanban board"
}

### 9. Read the imported plan

GET {{baseUrl}}/api/calendar/events/range?start=2099-01-12&end=2099-01-18

### 10. Create a break. moveToNextNonBreakDay is false: true would move every user's events that day.

# @name createCalendarBreak
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/calendar/breaks/create
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "date": "2099-01-09",
  "name": "HTTP test break",
  "moveToNextNonBreakDay": false
}

### 11. Is that date a break day? Expect {"isBreakDay": true}

GET {{baseUrl}}/api/calendar/breaks/is-break-day?date=2099-01-09

### 12. Rename the break

PUT {{baseUrl}}/api/calendar/breaks/{{createCalendarBreak.response.body.$.break.id}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "HTTP test break (renamed)"
}

### 13. Clean up: delete the break

DELETE {{baseUrl}}/api/calendar/breaks/{{createCalendarBreak.response.body.$.break.id}}

### 14. Clean up: delete the event from step 2

DELETE {{baseUrl}}/api/calendar/delete/{{addCalendarEvent.response.body.$.id}}

### 15. Clean up: delete the bulk and imported events by title (this removes every user's events with these titles)

DELETE {{baseUrl}}/api/calendar/delete_events
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "titles": ["HTTP test: unit 3 quiz", "HTTP test: retrospective", "HTTP test: plan review", "HTTP test: check-ins"]
}
